diff --git a/doc/source/whatsnew/v3.1.0.rst b/doc/source/whatsnew/v3.1.0.rst
index 16c15149a505c..c5c88182064b6 100644
--- a/doc/source/whatsnew/v3.1.0.rst
+++ b/doc/source/whatsnew/v3.1.0.rst
@@ -1150,6 +1150,7 @@ Sparse
 - Bug in :attr:`Series.sparse.density`, :attr:`DataFrame.sparse.density`, and :attr:`SparseArray.density` raising ``ZeroDivisionError`` on empty data instead of returning ``nan`` (:issue:`68468`)
 - Bug in :class:`SparseArray` and :class:`SparseDtype` accepting ``datetime64`` and ``timedelta64`` units pandas cannot hold: an unsupported resolution in the data is now cast to a supported one and an unsupported ``dtype=`` now raises, both matching the dense constructors, where previously a multiplier unit such as ``M8[10s]`` built a column that could not be displayed (:issue:`68522`)
 - Bug in :class:`SparseArray` with a datetime64 or timedelta64 subtype where a ``fill_value`` given as a :class:`Timestamp` or :class:`Timedelta` was silently truncated to microsecond resolution by :meth:`SparseArray.to_dense`, :meth:`SparseArray.value_counts`, :meth:`SparseArray.unique`, and arithmetic, and made ``np.asarray`` return ``object`` dtype holding raw integers, which the repr printed in place of the values; all of these behaved correctly for an equivalent ``np.datetime64`` fill value (:issue:`68589`)
+- Bug in :class:`SparseArray` with a datetime64 or timedelta64 subtype where a ``np.nan`` or ``pd.NA`` ``fill_value`` made ``np.asarray`` return an ``object`` array whose gaps held the fill value instead of ``NaT``, and whose remaining entries came back as raw integers on a nanosecond unit; :meth:`SparseArray.unique` raised ``ValueError``, and for a ``pd.NA`` fill value :meth:`SparseArray.to_dense` raised ``ValueError`` and comparison operations raised ``TypeError`` (:issue:`68558`)
 - Bug in :class:`SparseArray` with a datetime64 or timedelta64 subtype where a ``pd.NaT`` ``fill_value`` raised ``TypeError`` from :meth:`SparseArray.to_dense`, :meth:`SparseArray.unique`, :meth:`SparseArray.astype`, :meth:`SparseArray.take`, and arithmetic and comparison operations, and made ``np.asarray`` on a timedelta64 subtype return ``object`` dtype; all of these behaved correctly for an equivalent ``np.datetime64("NaT")`` fill value (:issue:`68449`)
 - Bug in :class:`SparseDtype` comparing equal to any dtype with the same subtype and a fill value of the same Python type once either side's fill value was NA, e.g. ``Sparse[float64, nan] == Sparse[float64, 1.5]``; among other things this made :meth:`SparseArray.astype` to such a dtype silently return the original array (:issue:`68582`)
 - Bug in :class:`SparseDtype` where two equal instances with an NA ``fill_value`` could hash differently, so a datetime64 or timedelta64 :class:`SparseDtype` could not be used as a dict key and identical sparse columns were counted separately by :meth:`Series.value_counts` on :attr:`DataFrame.dtypes` (:issue:`68449`)
